; Mirror's Edge VR - optional settings
;
; Rename this file to  mevr.ini  and leave it beside d3d9.dll in the game's Binaries folder.
; That is the whole installation: d3d9.dll and mevr.ini in the same directory.
;
; It is also read from %LOCALAPPDATA%\MirrorsEdgeVR\ (beside mevr.log) if you would rather not
; put files in the game folder. Beside the DLL wins if both exist, and the log names the file
; it actually read.
;
; With no file present every setting keeps its compiled default.
;
; Almost everything here can also be set by a hotkey in game, and the hotkey wins - so a run
; without this file can reach the same states.
;
; D3D9Ex is the exception, and cannot be otherwise: a graphics device's type is fixed when it
; is created, so there is no moment later at which a key could change it.
;
; Every line is reported in the log at startup - applied, or rejected with the reason. A
; misspelt key is never silent:
;
;     [cfg] ...the full path of the file it read, so there is never any doubt which one...
;     [cfg]   FrameCap = 72
;     [cfg]   unknown key 'FrmeCap' - ignored
;     [cfg] 1 applied, 1 ignored. Hotkeys still override anything set here.
;
; Binaries is safe. TdGame\Config is NOT - Mirror's Edge hash-checks the files in there and
; refuses to start when they are edited, which cost two runs to learn. Nothing in this file
; belongs anywhere near it.


; ---------------------------------------------------------------- debugging

; On by default. Turn it off for normal play and the mod becomes invisible: no text on the
; screen, and no hotkey does anything.
;
; Three keys stay live, because none of them is a diagnostic and each one leaves you stuck
; if it is missing:
;
;   PAGE UP      recentre the view. A headset put on at an angle needs this, and needs it
;                more with debugging off, since nothing else can correct the seating.
;   PAUSE (hold) close the game cleanly, so the engine writes its save.
;   F6           rescan for the view matrix. Stereo arms itself, but if it gives up it says
;                "press F6 by hand" - so the key has to still be there to press.
;
; Everything else - the overlay, the stereo strength and IPD controls, the animation locks,
; the frame cap, the capture and occlusion experiments - is off.
Debug = on


; ---------------------------------------------------------------- Phase 1 motion hands (experimental)
;
; The pose-input foundation is under active development. MotionHands currently samples the
; controller grip poses but does not move Faith's arms yet. Leave it OFF for ordinary play.
; MotionHandsDebug adds bounded pose validity/tracking transitions and a five-second report of
; grip/aim positions relative to the headset to mevr.log.

MotionHands = off
MotionHandsDebug = off


; ---------------------------------------------------------------- freeing the two grips
;
; On a Touch pair every button is already spoken for, and the two grips are the only controls
; that reach jump and quick turn. Anything that wants the grips for itself has to move those two
; actions first. These are the two settings that do it, kept separate because they answer
; different questions.
;
; StickJumpTurn puts jump on the right stick pushed UP and quick turn on the right stick pushed
; DOWN. It costs the pad's pitch axis, which is the one stick action a headset already does
; better - your head carries the pitch, and the game's own pitch is fought all frame by the view
; path anyway. Nothing else moves: turn stays on the right stick's X, and the grips keep working
; too, so with this on alone there are simply two ways to do each action.
;
; GripToGrip hands the grips to the hand instead: squeeze to close Faith's fingers, let go to
; open them. While it is on the grips no longer reach the game at all - a hand closing around
; nothing must not also jump - so it needs StickJumpTurn, and turns it on for you if you forgot.
; Turning StickJumpTurn on by itself is fine and changes nothing else.
;
; The hand closes proportionally, not on a threshold: half a squeeze is a half-closed hand. The
; fist itself was captured from Faith's own first-person run cycle, so it is the pose her
; animators authored for this mesh, thumb included, rather than something approximated here.
;
; Each hand is independent, and both stand down wherever the game poses the hands itself - a
; ledge, a pipe, a zipline, a melee - so a squeeze never fights an authored grab.

StickJumpTurn = off
GripToGrip = off


; ---------------------------------------------------------------- arm swing locomotion (measuring)
;
; Swing your arms to run, raise both hands to jump, crouch to crouch. None of that is built yet.
;
; What is here is rung AS.0, which MEASURES and moves nothing: it works out whether a real arm
; swing can be told apart from a head turn, a body turn, a gesture and a crouch, by logging four
; candidate ways of scoring the motion. The pad is not touched, so a run with this on plays
; exactly as it does with it off.
;
; ArmSwingDebug is the one that produces the data - a line a second, to mevr.log. Leave both off
; unless you are helping with the measurement.
;
; Hotkey: NUMPAD * toggles ArmSwing.

ArmSwing = off
ArmSwingDebug = off

; The two gestures, independently switchable because they fail differently.
;
; Jump needs BOTH hands clearly above your head - one hand never gets there during a swing, so
; the gesture cannot be produced by accident while running.
;
; Crouch measures your head against a standing height it works out for itself over the first
; couple of seconds. PAGE UP re-seeds it, so recentre while standing. Turn it off if you play
; seated: a seated baseline makes leaning forward look like a crouch.
;
; Turning Crouch off still leaves the swing suppressed while you squat - that is not this
; setting, it is the metric refusing to read a dropping head as arm motion.

ArmSwingJump = on
ArmSwingCrouch = on


; ---------------------------------------------------------------- frame pacing

; The engine's MaxSmoothedFrameRate. NUMPAD7 cycles 60 / 72 / 90 / 120 / 144 / 250 / 62.
;
; What matters is not the number but whether it divides the headset's refresh EXACTLY: a
; frame held for 2 display periods every time is smoother than a faster rate held for 2
; then 3. At 120 Hz that means 60; at 144 Hz it means 72. Press NUMPAD7 in game and the log
; reports the ratio for the headset actually attached.
;
; 62 is the game's own shipped default, kept for comparison. 250 is effectively uncapped.
FrameCap = 60


; ---------------------------------------------------------------- animation locks
;
; Mirror's Edge moves the camera by itself constantly - wall-run roll, landing dips, vaults.
; These cancel that motion per axis, leaving the view where your head put it.
;
; All OFF by default, which means the animation plays as the game shipped it. Turning one ON
; locks that axis against the animation.
;
; Yaw is the one to leave off in most cases: an animation that turns the player is carrying
; them somewhere, and cancelling it leaves the body facing one way and the view another.
;
; Hotkeys: NUMPAD2 pitch, NUMPAD4 roll, NUMPAD5 yaw.

LockAnimPitch = off
LockAnimRoll  = off
LockAnimYaw   = off


; ---------------------------------------------------------------- graphics path

; How the frame reaches the headset. Both of these exist for one reason: if the fast path
; misbehaves on your machine, you need a way back that does not require a different build.
;
; On by default, and worth leaving on - together they are what took the frame grab from about
; 4 ms to 0.4 and the frame rate from 75-80 to over 100.

; Ask Windows for a D3D9Ex device instead of the plain one the game requests. Everything below
; depends on it: a shared surface is an Ex feature, so D3D9Ex = off also forces FastCapture off.
;
; No hotkey, and there cannot be one - see the note at the top. If the game will not start or
; renders nothing at all, this is the first thing to turn off.
;
; An empty file named  mevr_noex.txt  beside d3d9.dll does the same thing and beats this
; setting. It exists for the case where the game fails before it gets far enough to be worth
; editing anything, and because an empty file can be created from a file manager without
; opening a text editor.
D3D9Ex = on

; Copy the frame on the GPU rather than dragging it through system memory. About ten times
; faster, and the only thing that could go wrong with it is tearing - two devices share one
; surface, and if the ordering between them ever fails you would see a torn strip rather than
; a wrong image.
;
; Hotkey: NUMPAD8, so you can compare both in one session.
FastCapture = on


; ---------------------------------------------------------------- the half-res hunt (diagnostics)
;
; Some runs come up soft: the engine quietly renders part or all of the scene into its
; 1280x720 buffer set instead of the full-resolution one, and the picture looks like 720p
; upscaled. Every log now carries a [mode] line per 12-second window - "healthy" or
; "DEGRADED" - plus [rt-first]/[surf]/[blit] lines that show which buffers the engine chose.
; The four keys below are levers for reproducing it on the monitor, with no headset. All of
; them default off and none belongs in normal play.
;
; TestStall injects ten 400 ms frames at the given frame number (0 = off) - an artificial
; load hitch. TestWideFov performs the same culling-FOV widening a headset run performs.
; OcclusionMode is the DELETE hotkey's setting made persistent: 0 auto, 1 every occlusion
; query reports visible, 2 never override. PinMinDesiredFps holds the engine's
; Client.MinDesiredFrameRate at zero. Measured 2026-08-28: slow frames, stalls, occlusion
; and the wide FOV each stay healthy alone on the monitor - the remaining suspects engage
; only in a real headset run, which these lines exist to diagnose when it next happens.
;
; The visible symptom - hands and building edges streaking along their motion history - is
; duplication running while the engine is in the half-res state, and it is guarded
; automatically: the mod drops to a clean mono view within a few seconds of detecting it
; and resumes stereo on its own if the engine recovers. No setting: the guard has no off
; switch because a run with it off has nothing to recommend it.

TestStall = 0
TestWideFov = off
OcclusionMode = 0
PinMinDesiredFps = off


; ---------------------------------------------------------------- beam balance
;
; On a narrow beam the game tips you and expects a counter on the strafe stick. This lets you
; lean your head instead, which is quicker to start than moving a thumb - and the game gives you
; only 0.8 s to answer before you fall.
;
; Head roll is the one axis of head tracking the game never sees, so this collides with nothing.
; The stick keeps working either way.
;
; If leaning tips you the WRONG way, flip BEAM SIGN in the F3 overlay (NUMPAD . to select,
; NUMPAD + or - to change). The handedness is measured rather than assumed.

BalanceHeadRoll = on
